{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_5pvdkkyjalpfuqhfx2ewvv426jkvdcsarosxwtfjmxnttzdhofea","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete. Summary:\n\n## Phase A — both programs build and run\n- **C:** `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `./c_src/build/driver` (gcc 11.5, no `-O`, non-PIE at `0x400000`)\n- **Rust:** `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `./translation/target/release/driver` (compiled with no errors as-is)\n\n## Phase B/C — one real mismatch found and fixed\nThe translation models the C program's data segment so it can reproduce the out-of-bounds table reads that `stb_perlin_noise3_wrap_nonpow2` performs (its `x0 = px % wrap; if (x0<0) x0 += wrap` stays *negative* for negative wrap arguments). It returned **zeroes** for every byte below `stb__perlin_randtab` — but in the real process `.data`, `.got`, `.dynamic`, `.rodata` and code live there:\n\n```\n$ printf '5 -133797.29 0.5 -542121.43 -164 -955542 197 5 INF -1e+38 355948.89 1' | driver\nC:    -0.0777234733     Rust (before): -0.141512632\n```\n\nFixed by embedding `src/c_data_image.bin` — a byte-for-byte copy of `0x400000..0x406000` of the reference C process, captured from `/proc/<pid>/mem` while it was blocked in `scanf` (regenerable via `tools/capture_c_data_image.sh`, and checked against the transcribed tables by a unit test).\n\nAlso worth flagging: I initially built the C with `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`, which moves the tables into `.rodata` and reorders them; that build disagrees with the un-optimized one on NaN signs and fault boundaries. The translation is calibrated to the **documented** (no build type) recipe, which is what I verified against.\n\n## Phase D — gate\n- `cargo test` passes in **both** debug and release: 8 differential tests (~500 inputs) + 1 unit test, nothing `#[ignore]`d.\n- Tests compare stdout, stderr **and** exit status (code *and* signal) by spawning both binaries; the Rust code is never loaded as a library. Coverage includes empty input, every field-count prefix 1..12, all glibc `%f` accept/reject paths, `%d` saturation/truncation, the `default: return NAN` arm, `octaves` 0/negative/256-wrap, and the **SIGFPE** (`INT_MIN % -1`) and **SIGSEGV** (read past the last mapped page) death paths.\n- Negative controls confirmed the suite bites: changing `%.9g` to `%.8g` failed 5 tests, and dropping the SIGSEGV emulation failed `nonpow2_wrap`.\n- ~20 000 additional random inputs (incl. a mode that deliberately drives indices out of bounds) show 0 mismatches.\n- `c_src/` sources are untouched (only `c_src/build/` was created, as instructed).\n\nOne caveat documented in `translation/ERRORS.md`: a few bytes in the mapped image are ASLR-randomised libc pointers, so inputs whose out-of-bounds reads land on them make the **C program itself** nondeterministic (e.g. `5 -82114 inf -1.69 -45 -257 -275 286` → 47/60 runs print `-nan`, 13/60 die with SIGSEGV). No translation can match those, so they are excluded from the suite; every out-of-bounds case that *is* checked in was verified stable over 16 consecutive C runs."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":373,"cache_read_input_tokens":156512,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":373,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4f1a6bb1-77a1-4ee5-b2c3-fe252a860f67","uuid":"b4bec901-1d30-43de-8886-0fc9abc3851d","timestamp":"2026-08-28T21:44:45.512Z"}
